---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Kenji-style French toast | Recipes">
  <article class="recipe">
    <header class="recipe-header">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/recipes">← All recipes</a></p>
      <h1>Kenji-style French toast</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The trick is the same one J. Kenji López-Alt has been preaching for
        years: thick slices of slightly stale, rich bread, a properly seasoned
        custard, a long enough soak that the bread is saturated all the way
        through, and a finishing pass in the oven so the centers cook
        through without scorching the outsides.
      </p>
      <ul class="recipe-meta">
        <li><strong>Yield</strong> · 4 servings (8 slices)</li>
        <li><strong>Active</strong> · 25 minutes</li>
        <li><strong>Total</strong> · ~45 minutes</li>
      </ul>
    </header>

    <section class="recipe-section">
      <h2>Ingredients</h2>
      <ul class="ingredients">
        <li>8 slices day-old challah or brioche, cut about ¾&quot; thick</li>
        <li>4 large eggs</li>
        <li>1 cup (240 ml) whole milk</li>
        <li>½ cup (120 ml) heavy cream</li>
        <li>3 tablespoons granulated sugar</li>
        <li>1 teaspoon vanilla extract</li>
        <li>½ teaspoon kosher salt</li>
        <li>¼ teaspoon ground cinnamon</li>
        <li>Pinch of freshly grated nutmeg</li>
        <li>4 tablespoons unsalted butter, divided</li>
        <li>Maple syrup and powdered sugar, to serve</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="recipe-section">
      <h2>Method</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          Heat the oven to 350°F (175°C) and set a wire rack inside a rimmed
          baking sheet.
        </li>
        <li>
          In a wide, shallow dish, whisk the eggs until fully smooth and
          uniform — no streaks of white. Whisk in the milk, cream, sugar,
          vanilla, salt, cinnamon, and nutmeg.
        </li>
        <li>
          Working two slices at a time, lay the bread in the custard. Soak
          for 60–90 seconds per side, pressing very gently so the custard
          works all the way to the center. Lift to the rack and let any
          excess drip off.
        </li>
        <li>
          Melt 1 tablespoon of butter in a 12-inch nonstick or well-seasoned
          cast-iron skillet over medium heat. Once the foam subsides, add 2
          soaked slices and cook until deeply golden, 2–3 minutes. Flip and
          cook 2 more minutes on the second side, then return to the rack.
        </li>
        <li>
          Repeat with the remaining butter and bread, lowering the heat a
          notch if the pan is darkening between rounds.
        </li>
        <li>
          When all slices are seared, slide the rack and sheet pan into the
          oven for 4–5 minutes. This sets the custard in the middle without
          drying out the crust.
        </li>
        <li>
          Dust with powdered sugar and serve immediately with warm maple
          syrup.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="recipe-section">
      <h2>Notes</h2>
      <ul class="notes">
        <li>
          Stale bread is not optional — it&apos;s the whole point. Fresh
          bread turns to mush in custard. If your loaf is fresh, slice it
          and dry the slices on a rack in a 200°F oven for 15 minutes.
        </li>
        <li>
          Cooking on a wire rack between batches lets steam escape so the
          crust stays crisp instead of going soggy.
        </li>
        <li>
          For a savory variation, drop the sugar and spices, add a few
          twists of black pepper to the custard, and serve with a fried
          egg and bacon.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
